(Appendix) Tj T*
(Here are methodological details on all of the measurements we've prototyped.) Tj T*
(Measuring AI-led R&D) Tj T*
(How we did it. The Automation Index requires three things: a complete map of all the AI R&D) Tj T*
(tasks being done at Anthropic, a way to rate the level of automation, and a way to weight the) Tj T*
(tasks, so that important areas of work count for more than less important ones. No one person) Tj T*
(can list every AI R&D task at a frontier AI company by hand, at least not at the granularity we) Tj T*
(want. Instead we constructed this list of tasks in a bottom-up manner from work records) Tj T*
(including Slack and various sources of internal documentation.) Tj T*
(For each week in July 2026, we randomly sampled 20% of staff from each department that make up) Tj T*
(the model R&D loop. A Claude research agent reviewed each sampled person's week using Slack and) Tj T*
(internal documentation, and listed the tasks they worked on. Repeating this for each week in) Tj T*
(July 2026 gives us a flat list of ~15,000 granular model R&D tasks. We then used Claude to) Tj T*
(organize these tasks into a hierarchical tree, starting from all model R&D at the root and) Tj T*
(branching into areas such as training and product, then pretraining and reinforcement learning,) Tj T*
(and so on down to increasingly specific kinds of work. The resulting tree has 542 nodes at) Tj T*
(different depths, of which 378 are leaves like "eval platform defect diagnosis and fixes," "RL) Tj T*
(sandbox egress and network policy," and "serving incident postmortems." We freeze this tree so) Tj T*
(that every measurement we make happens against the same basket of work.) Tj T*
(For each node in the tree \(a task category describing all the work beneath it\), a Claude agent) Tj T*
(deeply researches how that kind of work is done across the company: who does it, with what) Tj T*
(tools, and how much of it AI performs. An independent Claude judge then read the resulting) Tj T*
(evidence and assigned one of six automation levels, adopting a scale proposed by Epoch AI to) Tj T*
(differentiate the degree to which AI is used: no AI involvement, minimal AI involvement, AI) Tj T*
(assists, collaborates, leads, or is autonomous. When we rate a given month's automation, we) Tj T*
(only allow the research agents that do the ratings to see evidence from that month or earlier.) Tj T*
(To aggregate all the automation level ratings into one number, we want to give each node in the) Tj T*
(tree a weight corresponding to how important that work is to the overall model R&D effort.) Tj T*
(Rather than deciding ourselves what kinds of work are more important than others, we used the) Tj T*
(amount of person-time dedicated to that task as a proxy. Using our sample, we had Claude) Tj T*
(research what each person worked on during each week of July 2026. Each person gets one unit of) Tj T*
(weight per week, split evenly across the tasks they worked on that week. If person A worked on) Tj T*
(four tasks, each gets 0.25; if person B worked on ten, each gets 0.10. A category's weight is) Tj T*
(the sum of all the person-time weights given to it. This is a crude approximation, but on) Tj T*
(average the scheme behaves sensibly: it assigns more weight to categories that many people are) Tj T*
(assigned to.) Tj T*
(What this does and doesn't capture. First, the automation ratings depend on the judge model. To) Tj T*
(check them, we asked Anthropic staff who own the relevant work areas to rate the relative) Tj T*
(automation of their areas. To ensure an unbiased read, staff made their ratings without knowing) Tj T*
(what evidence the models had gathered or how they had judged that evidence. Our judge model) Tj T*
(agreed with humans about as often as humans agreed with each other \(model-versus-human exact) Tj T*
(agreement was 59%, human-versus-human was 35%\), and model and human ratings were within one) Tj T*
(level of each other 97% of the time. There remains real room for disagreement on borderline) Tj T*
(cases, such as where exactly "AI collaborates" ends and "AI leads" begins.) Tj T*
(Second, the basket is frozen. A growing index number on a July 2026 baseline tells us that the) Tj T*
(work humans were doing at that time is being automated; it does not, on its own, tell us) Tj T*
(whether new kinds of work are appearing that humans have shifted onto. To investigate this, we) Tj T*
(constructed an alternate version of the frozen tree from January 2026 data, and compared new) Tj T*
(tasks arriving every month from February to July 2026 against that January 2026 basket. We) Tj T*
(found no rise in the number of "novel" tasks between the January and July baskets of tasks,) Tj T*
(suggesting that the structure of model R&D work is stable at our level of analysis.) Tj T*
ET
